{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a0977"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge sport-games/ball-sports","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/sport-games__ball-sports.g3.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",598],["-","300,"],["+","300"],["=",1],["+","points, "],["=",514],["+","Serious bowlers buy their ball undrilled and have the holes drilled to the measured span of their own hand, usually as a fingertip grip, in which the middle and ring fingers go into the ball only up to the first knuckle; the house balls at the alley have the deeper conventional drilling instead. The biggest stage of all is in Japan: the Inazawa Grand Bowl near Nagoya, opened in 1972, has 116 lanes and is recognised as the world's largest bowling alley. "],["=",61]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",1090],["+","Ernsthafte Bowler kaufen ihre Kugel ungebohrt und lassen die Löcher nach der vermessenen Spanne ihrer Hand bohren, meist als Fingertip-Bohrung, bei der Mittel- und Ringfinger nur bis zum ersten Fingergelenk in der Kugel stecken; die Hausbälle der Bahn haben dagegen die tiefere konventionelle Bohrung. Die größte Bühne von allen steht in Japan: Der 1972 eröffnete Inazawa Grand Bowl bei Nagoya hat 116 Bahnen und gilt als größtes Bowlingcenter der Welt. "],["=",67]]}]},
{"seq":3,"at":"2026-10-21","kind":"corrected","target":{"type":"article","id":"a0977"},"reason":"a0977 body: \"bouncing one pin off the side wall into the other, and even professionals succeed only a few times in a hundred attempts\" → \"driving one pin into the other, usually by making it rebound off the side wall or out of the pit, and even professionals seldom succeed\" (EN/DE) — unsourced rate; Wikipedia names wall and pit — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Split_(bowling)","by":"enrich batch 196","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-enrich/enrich-report/a0977-a1481.md","sources":[{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Split_(bowling"}]},
